  "query_preview": "..\nToday into tonight: As the fog continues to mix out from the AM \nhours, an increasing wind gradient will aid in mixing out any \nremaining fog. Current NW flow aloft associated with a incoming\ntrough. Satellite observations continue to exhibit a persistent\nlow stratus deck that is forecast to linger throughout most of \nthe weekend. While a high of 36-38 degrees is expected today, it \nmay come up a bit short due to the lack of daytime heating. \nAlongside cloud cover mitigating daytime warming, exists \nevaporative cooling with potential precipitation falling then \nevaporating, thus decreasing temperatures further. \n\nFriday PM through Sunday night: Continued NWP guidance has\nconsistently similar messaging. Large scale upper troughing \ncontinues across the western US the remainder of the week end. The\nnext system will bring another shot of cold, Canadian air this \nweekend. A cut-off low is forecast to eject into the main flow and\nwill bring a weather system to the region beginning Saturday \nmorning. This could generate isolated chances for snow around the \nedges of the CWA. However, the main impact will be the below \nnormal temperatures with cooler wind chills post-frontal passage. \nWind chills this weekend are forecast to be in the teens to single\ndigits during the AM hours. Coldest wind chills exist between 6-8\nAM during the weekend and into the work week next week - \npotentially into the morning of Tuesday.\n\nMonday to Wednesday: Troughing with northwest flow aloft, then\nridging thereafter. Conditions will return to near seasonal norms\nand remain relatively dry. By Monday AM, an upper-level trough \nwill bring colder temperatures which may elevate PoPs enough to \nallow for some additional light snow. Wednesday depicts \nimpressive, high heights in which a ridge will build during this\nperiod. This will be accompanied by warmer than average \ntemperatures.\n\nWednesday night onward: While there a larger spread in potential\noutcomes with models disagreeing by this point in time, GEFS and \nEPS ensembles are pointing towards ridging conditions with warmer\ntemperatures existing until 29th towards the 30th. Temperatures\nduring this period have a chance to become higher than climate\nnormals. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\n- High confidence for light snow and/or flurries today through \n  Monday night (70%).\n\n- Low confidence Wednesday night onward (25%). GEFS and EPS\n  members agreed among ridging conditions however, the exact\n  timing and intensity of the ridge aloft is uncertain. \n\n- Moderate confidence in temperatures after Monday (35%-50%).\n  \n\nFINDLEY/GAH",
